<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Walter Raleigh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1552–<a href="/years/1618">1618</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Sir Walter Raleigh, Walter Ralegh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Walter Raleigh (c. 1552–1618) was an English courtier, soldier, explorer and writer, a favourite of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a>. He sponsored the failed Roanoke colony in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, and in 1595 sailed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> and up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orinoco">Orinoco</a> in search of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-dorado">El Dorado</a>. Governor of Jersey from 1600, he was imprisoned by James I in 1603, wrote a History of the World in the Tower, and was executed in 1618 after a second Orinoco expedition attacked a Spanish settlement.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/walter-raleigh.jpg" alt="Walter Raleigh" width="320" height="394" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_Walter_Ralegh_by_%27H%27_monogrammist.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Raleigh was born into a landed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> family of Protestant faith at Hayes Barton, in East Devon, the youngest of five sons of Walter Raleigh of Fardel Manor. His mother, Katherine Champernowne, was the third wife of his father. His half-brothers included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humphrey-gilbert">Humphrey Gilbert</a> and Adrian Gilbert, and through the Champernowne connection the Raleigh and Gilbert brothers all became prominent under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-i">Elizabeth I</a> and James I. Little is known of his early life, though in his late teens he spent some time in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> taking part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-wars-of-religion">religious civil wars</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1579 to late 1580 Raleigh took part in suppressing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desmond-rebellions">Desmond Rebellions</a> in Ireland and was present at the siege of Smerwick. In 1585 he received 40,000 acres in the Munster Plantation, including Youghal, where he was mayor in 1588 and 1589. A royal charter of 25 March 1584 authorised him to explore and colonise lands not held by any Christian prince, and the region reached by the first expedition was named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virginia">Virginia</a> for Elizabeth. He was knighted in 1585. In 1591 he secretly married Elizabeth Throckmorton, one of the queen's ladies-in-waiting, and the couple were imprisoned in the Tower of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in 1592.</p><p>Raleigh heard of a city of gold in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> and explored what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guyana">Guyana</a> and eastern Venezuela in search of Lake Parime and Manoa. His Discovery of Guiana, published in 1596, made exaggerated claims and contributed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-dorado">El Dorado</a> legend. He took part in the capture of Cádiz in 1596 and in the Islands Voyage of 1597, and from 1600 to 1603 as governor of Jersey he modernised its defences, including the building of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-castle">Elizabeth Castle</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Arrested on 19 July 1603 for involvement in the Main Plot against James I, Raleigh defended himself at his trial at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winchester">Winchester</a>. The chief evidence was the sworn confession of Henry Brooke, 11th Baron Cobham, whom the tribunal refused to call as a witness. He was convicted, but the king spared his life. Pardoned in 1617, he led a second expedition to Venezuela, during which men under Lawrence Kemys attacked the Spanish outpost of Santo Tomé de Guayana, and his son Walter was killed. On his return the Spanish ambassador demanded that the sentence be reinstated, and Raleigh was beheaded at the Old Palace Yard on 29 October 1618. His poetry, in the plain style, includes The Nymph's Reply to the Shepherd.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Walter Raleigh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…to a golden city and a lost kingdom, drew expeditions into the Llanos, the Amazon and the Orinoco for more than a century, among them <b>Sir Walter Raleigh</b>'s voyage to Guiana in 1595, and treasure-hunters tried to drain Lake Guatavita from the sixteenth century to the early twentieth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/muisca-confederation">Muisca Confederation · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Courtier, poet and explorer <b>Sir Walter Raleigh</b> was executed in Old Palace Yard under a treason sentence from 1603.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-29">29 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Walter Raleigh</b> burnt it in 1595.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-trinidad">Spanish Trinidad</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hayes Barton, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.66,-3.35&amp;year=1594" aria-label="Hayes Barton on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4474 405 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Walter Raleigh"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4924 675h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4924 675h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.66,-3.35&amp;year=1594">What was here in 1594? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Even such is time, that takes in trust Our youth, our joys, our all we have, And pays us but with earth and dust; Who in the dark and silent grave, When we have wandered all our ways, Shuts up the story of our days…&#8221;</p><footer>Lines written the night before his execution, 1618 &#183; original &#183; as quoted in Edmund Gosse's life of him (1886) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/27580/pg27580-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…a sharp medicine, but one that will cure me of all my diseases.&#8221;</p><footer>Words on the scaffold, 29 October 1618, touching the axe &#183; original &#183; as reported by the witnesses, in Edmund Gosse's life of him (1886) &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/27580/pg27580-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…whosoever in writing a modern history, shall follow truth too near the heels, it may haply strike out his teeth.&#8221;</p><footer>The History of the World, preface (1614) &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/13182/pg13182-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I thank my God heartily that He hath brought me into the light to die, and not suffered me to die in the dark prison of the Tower…&#8221;</p><footer>Speech on the scaffold, 29 October 1618 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_3/His_Last_Words_on_the_Scaffold" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;But in this I speak now, what have I to do with kings? I have nothing to do with them, neither do I fear them. I have now to do with God…&#8221;</p><footer>Speech on the scaffold, 29 October 1618 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_3/His_Last_Words_on_the_Scaffold" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…having been a soldier, a captain, a sea captain, and a courtier, which are all places of wickedness and vice…&#8221;</p><footer>Speech on the scaffold, 29 October 1618 &#183; original &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_World%27s_Famous_Orations/Volume_3/His_Last_Words_on_the_Scaffold" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
